 the dynamic environment affect  biology  did all cells respond to the treatment  in the same way  the cells recover after the treatment  how and when did they recover  this is all important information to  help us view the big picture  and simple life cell imaging can help us  with that  so take autophagy in mammalian cells as  an example  when we start the cells they will form  these micro vesicles called autovegasm  to recycle the waste and to rebuild  proteins that will help them survive  through difficult times  like these videos clips on the left is  the control group  where cells are normal in normal medium  you wouldn't see the accumulation of the  autophagosomes  and on the right is when cells are  starving  in pbs you see more and more green dots  appear  at 40 minutes into the starvation  you see when and where they are formed  and by using lifestyle imaging  you're confident to say yes the cells  respond to starvation  and also the autophagy kicks in within  one minute  of starvation treatment  and now we use hypoxia as the stress  source  this time to initiate autophagy itself  then you plot the fluorescence intensity  over time in a chart and find different  dynamics in the cellular responses  and based on the piece of the data you  find that  at one point if you stretch the cells  with autophagy inhibitor  like chloroquine in this case and  prevent cells from  recycling and rebuilding the cells would  be so  stressed that even normoxia couldn't  bring the cells back to the normal state  these are all the findings endpoint 88  cannot  provide  here's another example of light cell  imaging experiments  so you grow mammalian cells on our  device in this case it's the human  colorectal  adrenal carcinoma ht29 cell line  and then you introduce the invasive e  coli to study when  and where the invasion occurs in this  case ht29 is stressed  by the invasive bacteria within 6  hours and that eventually wrapped her 15  minutes after the treatment  without light cell imaging you wouldn't  know how fast the invasion would take  place  so now we all agree lifestyle imaging is  important and it gives  you both spatial and temporal  information that endpoint  issues don't provide but how do we do  lifestyle imaging for cell cultures  you'll need a cell culture device to  hold your cells  you also need temperature and gas  control to keep cells healthy  for the images you'll need a microscope  there are some products out there to do  this but they're  huge and often expensive they basically  put a microscope in the incubators  but let's just say you don't care about  the size  and the price tag there's still  limitations  for example for a giant incubator like  this it takes six to eight hours to  reach equilibrium  after it reaches equilibrium you would  have to put the cells into the  incubators by  opening and closing the incubator doors  finger crossing that  by opening and closing the door the gas  content would remain the same  and some lifestyle imaging systems  allowed you to treat yourself  during your experiments but the tubing  going in and out of the incubators is  just too difficult to set up  so here comes our unique stylistic onyx  2 lifestyle imaging system  it's pretty small about the size of a  shoebox  it's compact as well it comes with a  reagent control system  a manifold that ensures temperature and  gas control in the cell culture chambers  and then a culture plate to hold  yourself  you load yourself onto the plate hook it  up with the manifold  and then you use your computer to  control the perfusion  gas and temperature and then you use  your microscope software to set up the  time-lapse imaging  under the microscope and then you click  on start  on both the perfusion system and your  computer  and then walked away you come back the  next day  and get data and it's simple at that  so when you set up experiments it's  going to look like this  you have the cell culture play and then  you have cells  and solutions loaded to the designated  wells  you hook it up with the manifold that  connects to the system  then you use the software to control the  pressures on the cell inlet well  so when the pressure is on the media or  the cell solutions  in that well is going to root through  the micro channels and go into the cell  culture chambers  this is how you load the cells or treat  the cells  once you're happy with the loading  results you can then set up the temp  treatment time the gas  and temperature on the onyx software  place the plate on the microscope and  use your microsoft software  to set up capturing intervals automate  autofocusing setting up beacon etc  and then press start on the onyx system  and also press start on the microscope  catch  capture button and now the system is  going to trick yourself  change gap content or change temperature  at certain time points all by  itself while your microscope is taking  pictures on the cell  the even more unique feature is that we  offer a wide range of microfluidic  plates  for different applications we have a  million plates  which allow you to culture mammalian  cells and then you can flow in bacteria  like the past host pathogen experiments  we previously talked about  we have eat plates and bacteria plates  that are designed  specifically to hold these microbes in  place over time  but no matter what plate type it is they  all have a standard 96 well plate frame  there are four units on each plate so  that you can use one as the control  and do three other experiments  do three other treatments in sd  experiment groups  there are six upstream reservoirs and  the bottom is cover slip glass  that is compatible with most of the  inverted microscopes  let's focus on the yeast and bacteria  plates we launched couple years ago  the main feature of these plates is that  there is  an elastic pdms layer on the top of the  cell culture chambers  when you load the cells with high flow  of media it pushes the seaming to the  top  when loading is done the ceiling drops  to certain heights  but narrow enough to gently hold the  cells in place  then even there's perfusion media flow  the cells will still remain in one focal  plane there are three tier ceilings  on y4c ranging from 3.5  4.5 to 5 micron y4d  ceilings are taller ranging from  5 six to seven micron four deploy cells  b4a ceiling heights ranging from 0.7.9  1.1 1.2 micron  so no matter what size your microbes are  they'll be trapped under one of the  tiered  ceilings and be holding place for the  entire time  without this trapping mechanism if  you're just taking an image on cells  cultured on other pads or on glass  slides  you simply cannot capture a shot that is  in focus  let alone monitoring the expressed or  labor proteins  in real time and that is definitely an  issue onyx  let's take a look at how researchers use  our y4c  plate here's a clip from one of our  customers  in japan used y4c plate to study  mechanisms  behind chromosomes and plasma dna  dynamics in the cell  they are also interested in monitoring  how cells form their  shapes  here's another testimonial another  researcher  professor springer at harvard use onyx  y4c plates to stimulate cells with two  treatments and see how cells decide what  to do  also they use the system to heat shock  the cells and see genes  turning on and off during perturbation  let's he hear him explain in his own  words  we're interested in how cells respond to  heterogeneous environments  so typically when we do things we have  we change media but  it has both input one and input two  there but we wanna see times if we have  like input one on one side of the cell  and input two on the other side of the  cell  how does it integrate that decision and  is it integrated differently than when  the cell  is is faced with a mixture of those two  inputs across the host cell  on some level nothing that we discovered  that we probably couldn't have  discovered a lot of hard work in 